course: english
lesson: 19
title: Prediction: what happens next
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A prediction is a sensible guess about what will happen next, built from clues in the text and from what you know about stories. «Sam saw the sign: DANGER: THIN ICE. He looked at the frozen pond and grinned.» The grin is the clue: Sam is probably going to step onto the ice, and stories love trouble, so he may well fall in.
explain: A prediction does not have to come true to be a good one. It has to be based on evidence. In a test that says «Predict what might happen next, using evidence from the text (2 marks)», you need two things: what you think will happen, and the clue that makes you think so, joined with «because».
explain: Good readers change their predictions as they go, like a weather forecaster who looks out of the window again. Kai changes his mind halfway through a book: that is not failing, it is reading well. And authors sometimes surprise us on purpose: Isla predicts the princess will save herself instead of waiting for a prince, and in many modern stories she does.
explain: Josh in Ohio says, «Bet he's gonna get caught, that's sus» (sus is short for suspicious). That is a real prediction in slang. In a written answer he would write: «I think he will get caught because he keeps looking over his shoulder.»
fact: A prediction is a sensible guess about what will happen next, based on clues.
fact: A good prediction gives what might happen and the evidence that suggests it.
fact: A prediction can earn marks even if the story turns out differently, as long as it fits the evidence.
fact: Wild guesses with no link to the text, like a dragon turning into a cake for no reason, are not good predictions.
fact: You can predict before reading, from the title, the cover and the pictures.
fact: Good readers revise their predictions as they find new clues.
fact: Knowing common story patterns helps: a character warned «never open the red door» very often opens it.
fact: Authors sometimes break the usual pattern to surprise the reader.
fact: A gasp, a scream or a dropped cup are clues that something surprising has happened.
fact: A spoiler is when someone tells you the ending before you get there.
fact: Predicting keeps you thinking about the story and makes reading more exciting.
fact: Slang: «sus» means suspicious; «bet» (US) can mean «I'm sure» or «OK»; «gonna» means going to; «mid» means average, nothing special.
exercise: «Sam saw the sign: DANGER: THIN ICE. He looked at the frozen pond and grinned.» What will Sam probably do? | Step onto the ice | Walk home and do his homework quietly in his bedroom | Call the police | Build a snowman far away from the pond
exercise: Freddie predicts the dragon will become a cake. What is wrong with that prediction? | Nothing in the text suggests it | Dragons can't be eaten | It is too short | Predictions must always be about cakes, so it's fine
exercise: Isla predicts the princess will save herself. Can a prediction like this be good? | Yes, if clues show the princess is brave | No, princesses in stories are always saved by princes | No, you can't predict anything about princesses | Only if the book is very old
exercise: A book cover shows a boy in a magic hat. What can you do before reading? | Predict it's about magic, then read to check | Nothing, you must never think about a book before reading every page of it | Skip to the end | Decide not to read it
exercise: Josh says, «Bet he's gonna get caught, that's sus.» What does sus mean? | Suspicious | Super | Sure | Sad because something bad has happened to him at home
exercise: «The phone rang. Mia picked it up and gasped.» What might Mia have heard? | Surprising or shocking news | Nothing, she was bored | A wrong number for a pizza shop, which she was expecting | Her own voice
exercise: Kai changes his prediction halfway through the book. Is that a mistake? | No, good readers update predictions with new clues | Yes, you must keep your first prediction to the end of the book | Yes, it means he didn't read properly | No, because predictions don't matter at all
exercise: «The old man handed Lily a key. "Never open the red door," he warned.» What do you predict? | Lily will open the red door | Lily will throw away the key and forget about it straight away | The old man will open it | Nothing will happen
exercise: Mum says, «Don't tell me the end, I want to guess.» Ella says, «Spoilers!» What is a spoiler? | Telling someone the ending too early | A word for a bad book | A kind of prediction | A broken toy that the dog chewed
exercise: «Predict what might happen next, using evidence from the text. (2 marks)» What do you need? | A prediction and a clue from the text | Just the prediction, written neatly | A long summary of everything so far | A drawing of the ending
exercise: What is the best base for a prediction? | Clues in the text and what you know about stories | A coin toss | What your friend said at lunch | The number of pages left in the book
exercise: Why do authors sometimes break the usual story pattern? | To surprise the reader | Because they forgot the pattern | Because patterns are banned in books | To make the book shorter
exercise: Which is a prediction? | I think the dog will find its way home | The dog is brown | The dog ran away on Monday, as the text says | I like dogs
exercise: Why can you predict from a book cover? | Titles and pictures give clues about the story | Covers always tell you the ending | Covers are written by the reader | The cover is the first chapter
test: «she gasped so she heard a joke». Is that the best prediction? | Not quite, a gasp usually shows shock or surprise | Yes, gasps always mean someone is laughing | Yes, any guess is as good as another | No, gasps mean she is bored
test: ★★ «Grace practised the piano every night for months. On the day of the concert, she walked onto the stage and took a deep breath.» What is a sensible prediction? | She plays well, because she practised so much | She forgets how to play and runs off crying, though nothing hints at it | She falls asleep | The piano turns into a horse
test: «bro I predicted the ending wrong so I failed». Did he fail? | No, a prediction is good if it fits the evidence | Yes, wrong predictions score zero | Yes, predictions must always come true | No, but only because he guessed
test: ★★ The old man warned «never open the red door». Halfway through, Lily throws the key into the sea. What should a good reader do? | Revise the prediction | Keep the old prediction, whatever happens in the story | Stop reading | Start the book again
test: ★★★ «Every night, the biscuit tin was emptier. Every morning, there were crumbs by the dog's bed, and the dog looked very pleased.» Which prediction uses the evidence best? | The family will find out the dog is eating the biscuits | The family will buy a new tin because the old one has a hole | The dog will bake more biscuits | A burglar will be caught next week
test: ★★★ A story has two clues: «Tom kept his hands in his pockets all day» and «Mum's best vase was missing from the shelf». Which prediction uses BOTH clues? | Tom broke the vase and is hiding a cut or the pieces | Tom is cold, so he will put his gloves on tomorrow | Mum will buy a new shelf | The vase will come back by itself
test: In a cartoon, a sign says WET PAINT and a character leans on the wall. What do you predict? | He gets paint all over himself | Nothing happens, because cartoons always skip the obvious bit | The wall disappears | The paint dries straight away
test: Gen Alpha cousin: «nah predicting is mid, just read». Why is predicting useful? | It keeps you thinking about the story and makes it exciting | It means you don't have to finish the book | It's only for tests and has no use at all | It lets you skip to the end
test: What would happen if a story always did exactly what you predicted? | It might feel boring | It would win every prize | It would have no words | You couldn't read it at all, because the words would vanish
test: Sam grinned at the thin ice sign. In real life, what is the safe thing to do near a THIN ICE sign? | Stay off the ice | Walk on the ice, but slowly | Jump on it to test it | Walk on it if a friend dares you to, as long as it looks frozen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a prediction in reading? | A reasoned anticipation of subsequent events, founded upon textual evidence | A recollection of events already narrated | An entirely random guess | A statement of the reader's preferences, without reference to the text at all
exam: A character, having been warned never to open a certain door, is handed its key. Identify the prediction most consistent with established narrative convention. | That the character will open the door | That the character will never think of the door again for the remainder of the story | That the key will vanish | That the door will be removed
exam: Ascertain the components of a complete two-mark answer to a prediction question. | A predicted event and supporting evidence from the text | A predicted event only | A summary of the whole book | A personal opinion of the author, offered without supporting evidence from the text
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the revision of predictions. | It is a mark of good reading when new evidence emerges | It is prohibited | It demonstrates a failure of comprehension on the part of the reader in every instance | It is permitted only before reading
